/-
Johnson/Desc/ProofFile/Written/Priced.lean: the plans of a proof file's three chains with the error
of every round, at the full-size and at the small example, as written values.

The planner makes these plans from the three public files by parsing, which Lean's kernel does not
evaluate. Here they stand written out: the distinct rounds of each chain once, and each plan as
runs. A pair (n, j) of a list of runs stands for n rounds in a row that are all record j, counted
from 0, of the same chain's list of records. The file holds definitions of data: no function, no
theorem, no evaluation.
Main names, with i = 0, 1, 2 for the first, the shared and the second chain:
  `pricedProofSetFull : Setting`: the setting of the three plans.
  `pricedProofRecsFulli : List PlanRound`: the chain's distinct rounds, each with all seven fields
    by position: entry, error, group, why, rests, scope, wordFault. An error stands as the two exact
    fields of `Binary`. Two rounds are one record just if all seven fields are the same. Each chain
    has its own list, so a round that two chains share stands twice.
  `pricedProofRunsFulli : List (Nat × Nat)`: the chain's plan as runs. Neighbours of one record are
    merged. A long list stands in parts (`…p0`, `…p1`, …) that are joined by `++`.
  The same names with `Small` for `Full`.
Whence the numbers: a program wrote the values from two prints that Lean's evaluator made of
`m.p.chains.made.proof` (`OnBytes.Params.Configured.proof`), the whole of each plan's `rounds` in
order. Here m is what the planner makes of policy.txt, layout.txt and KL.bin in the folder config/
of the stored examples examples/full-size and examples/accepted-proof. No other file was read for
these values. The prints say that the records laid out by the runs were the plan's rounds, as many
and field by field, and that `Johnson.G.OfPlan.madeOf` (Johnson/G/RoundsOfPlan.lean) and the proof
file's description's own planner gave the same setting and rounds. The program read its own file
back and found every record and every pair of the prints in it, in order.
Limits: this file by itself holds the values against nothing, and no theorem ties them to the
planner. No test that a build runs compares them with it. Nothing here says that an error bounds
what its round's name says. They are the plans at these two sets of public files and at no other.

-/
import OnBytes.Params.Planner

set_option autoImplicit false
